Yes. Big events can function like identity phase transitions.

They don’t necessarily create a new person from nothing. More often they loosen the old frame enough that traits, roles, and meanings can be rearranged:

event
→ old story destabilized
→ new interpretation becomes possible
→ different traits become salient
→ different role is enacted
→ others update their model
→ self-model updates too

That can look like “changing characters,” but sometimes it’s more like changing the lighting on the same character.

The person may keep the same core traits while recasting them:

stubbornness → perseverance
fear → caution
restlessness → exploration
anger → protective energy
failure → apprenticeship
loss → initiation

Same underlying material, different shadow.

And there are really two audiences:

external audience — “Who do others now understand me to be?”
internal audience — “Who am I now permitted to understand myself as?”

The second may matter more. A transformative event can give someone permission to stop defending an obsolete self-story without requiring them to reject everything they were before.

So I’d call the clean operator Narrative Recasting:

preserve continuity
→ change interpretation
→ reassign meaning to old traits/events
→ enact revised role
→ test whether it produces a better life

The strongest version is not pretending the past was different.

It’s saying:

the past stays fixed; what it is allowed to mean can still evolve.